# act-local-CI config (read only by the `act` CLI; GitHub Actions has no concept
# of this file, so it has zero effect on real CI). The global pre-push hook
# (installed via git's init.templateDir) runs `act push` on every push to a
# protected branch — these two lines make that run actually match ci.yml.
#
# 1. macos-latest -> -self-hosted
#    act only runs Linux containers, so the `build` job (runs-on: macos-latest:
#    swift build debug/release + build_app.sh) was being SKIPPED with
#    "🚧 Skipping unsupported platform" — silently, without failing. That left
#    the Swift build with no local coverage at all, and since cloud ci.yml is
#    disabled for this repo, a Swift regression would have reached main
#    uncaught. `-self-hosted` runs that job's steps natively on this Mac
#    instead of in a container, which is both faster and free — note ci.yml's
#    own comment that macos-latest bills at 10x on a private repo.
#    Caveat: -self-hosted means those steps run directly on the host with no
#    container isolation, and `swift build` writes .build/ into the working
#    tree (already gitignored).
#
# 2. --env PATH=...
#    Defensive, same fix as Stock-Trading's .actrc: under
#    --container-architecture linux/amd64 on Apple Silicon, the first
#    `::add-path::` in a job makes act rebuild PATH using the HOST arch and
#    point at a nonexistent .../node/<ver>/arm64/bin, so every later `node`
#    (including act's own post-step invocations for JS actions such as
#    actions/cache) dies with exit 127. Re-derive with
#    `docker run --rm --platform linux/amd64 catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest \
#     cat /etc/environment` if the image is ever bumped; drift fails loudly.
-P macos-latest=-self-hosted
--env PATH=/opt/acttoolcache/node/24.19.0/x64/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/games:/usr/local/games:/snap/bin
